#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088747} :activity/id #uuid "3a74e679-27e2-55e8-b796-41cc2e6cec8c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699 :topic/id #uuid "0c8c634b-da7d-56b9-a349-8bf570a72393" :topic/math-academy-id 849} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088913 :tutorial/content "The equation of an ellipse centered at the point $( {\\color{red}h}, {\\color{blue}k})$ with a horizontal radius of length $\\color{red}a$ and a vertical radius of length $\\color{blue}b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-{\\color{red}h})^2}{{\\color{red}a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-{\\color{blue}k})^2}{{\\color{blue}b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the equation that describes the ellipse below, centered at $({\\color{red}2},{\\color{blue}1}).$\n\n![](images/5c/5c65dc8e6c943fa46161b988aedd4ac126c2806278e571b33a2c4f98c4ea895e.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal radius has length ${\\color{red}a} = {\\color{red}4},$ while the vertical radius has length ${\\color{blue}b} = {\\color{blue}3}.$ \n\nSubstituting ${\\color{red}a} = {\\color{red}4},$ ${\\color{blue}b} = {\\color{blue}3},$ and $( {\\color{red}h}, {\\color{blue}k}) = ( {\\color{red}2}, {\\color{blue}1}),$ we find that the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "31649dfb-addc-5f11-981d-2b22593321ca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3413 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f8ba752d-2bb2-53c2-9558-2b9dd52d37ff" :step/math-academy-id 461515 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087529 :question/id #uuid "67da2ffa-b472-5084-985c-dde4cd2df661" :question/math-academy-id "e-5219"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge